  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 144 AM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nClear skies & calm winds are leading to a chilly night as\nCanadian high pressure settles over the region. Lows have\ndropped into the low 20s across much of the area, except across\npotions of western Wisconsin where stratus coming off of Lake\nSuperior has limited radiational cooling & kept temperatures\nsteady around 30 degrees. The chilly start to the day means\nwe'll see another day where afternoon highs remain in the 30s\ndespite plenty of sunshine, & another cold night is expected\nwith low in the upper teens to low 20s for Sunday morning.\nTemperatures begin to warm Sunday as southerly flow returns on\nthe backside of departing high pressure. The warmup really\ncommences early next week as upper-levl ridging builds over the\ncentral CONUS & ensemble guidance continues to high light\ntemperature anomalies of 15-20 degrees above normal by mid- \nweek. Much of this temperature signal will be felt during the \novernight hours with overnight lows expected to remain in the \n40s, but we could see some 60s across the area Wednesday & \nthursday afternoons deepening on the extent of cloud cover. Dry\nweather continues through at least midweek until a deepening \ntrough over the western US pushes the ridging off to our east. \nModels are still spread on the strength & track of any \ndisturbances that round the trough & pass through the Upper \nMidwest, & thus on the extent of precipitation chances for \nlater in the week. While it's hard to pin down exact \nprecipitation chances, we'll be in a more active pattern \nbeginning Thursday & continuing into next weekend so at least \nsome amount of precipitation, likely rain given forecast \ntemperatures, is likely through this period.",
